Statements:
[
    "A benchmark gets saturated when models start performing extremely well on it over time.",
    "The benchmark is no longer able to differentiate between models because they all achieve very high scores.",
    "Benchmark saturation happens because the benchmark remains static while models are continually improving.",
    "The benchmark becomes less useful for evaluating the performance of different models.",
    "Initially, models might score around 25% or 36% on a benchmark.",
    "As new models are developed and trained, their performance on the benchmark improves over time.",
    "Eventually, models reach scores of 90-95% or even higher.",
    "At this point, many models may be scoring similarly, making it difficult to determine which one is actually performing better.",
    "Benchmark saturation is a sign that the benchmark has become too easy for the current generation of models.",
    "Benchmark saturation means the benchmark is no longer providing useful insights into the relative strengths and weaknesses of models."
] 

